// 'sizeof' is deliberately NOT foldable by the forward scanner, and it is FALSE here ('int' is
// always 4 bytes), so the interface declaration is never compiled. The scanner cannot decide the
// branch, so it routes 'GiUnd<int>' as a fat pointer with no method table behind it.
//
// An earlier version of this test asserted that a METHOD CALL is the only thing that can go wrong,
// because a call resolves through the interface table and a missing entry makes it a clean compile
// error. That is true but NOT sufficient, and the difference is the reason this test exists: the
// name still lowers to a FAT POINTER, so even though you cannot call through it you CAN assign
// through it. It then launders one real interface's vtable into another unrelated real interface,
// because every validation predicate keys on "is this a known interface" - false for this name - so
// both the rebox and the impossible-conversion check are skipped. That produced a SIGTRAP in one
// variant and a silent wrong value with exit 0 in another.
//
// So the point that must be rejected is where a VALUE of the type is materialised. Rejecting the
// declaration closes every downstream use (assignment, 'is'/'as', field, argument) at once.
// NOTE on the expect_error FORM: this diagnostic is deferred to the END of the compile (it can only
// be decided once every generic instantiation has drained), so it fires after any scoped
// expect_error block has closed. The BARE FILE-SCOPE form is the one that stays armed to that point.
expect_error("was never instantiated");

if const (sizeof(int) == 8) {
    interface GiUnd<T> { T Get(); };
}

extern int main()
{
    GiUnd<int> u = default;
    return 0;
}
